+# bar-comparison
+
+## Purpose
+Compare 2 to 6 labelled, non-negative numbers as bars, for example the cost or latency of two designs.
+
+## Agent contract
+- Select `visual_type: bars` in a **curated** storyboard. The AI planner is not offered this skill, because it needs exact data.
+- Put the numbers in `data`, never in prose: `{"title": "Cost per run", "unit": "$", "bars": [{"label": "Medium model", "value": 0.0148}, {"label": "Cascade", "value": 0.0755}]}`.
+- Take every number from a tested source (for example the engine of a lab), not from a typed guess.
+- Use the existing trusted `studio.animation.build_scene` renderer. Do not invent arbitrary Manim Python APIs.
+- All factual explanations require independent review.
+
+## Supported input
+- `id`, `objective`, `narration`, `visual_type: bars`
+- `data.bars`: 2 to 6 items, each exactly `{"label": text, "value": number}`; labels unique, at most 24 characters, only letters, digits, space and `. , : % $ ( ) / + & -`; values finite and between 0 and 1,000,000,000
+- `data.title` (optional, at most 60 characters) and `data.unit` (`""`, `"$"`, `"ms"` or `"%"`)
+- `duration_seconds` (optional, 3 to 90): the scene lasts about as long as its narration
+
+## Limitations
+A starter template: one group of bars, no axis ticks, no grouped or stacked bars, English-style labels only. Anything outside the rules above is rejected, never escaped.
